.TH WOAD 3 "August 27, 2026" "woad @PROJECT_VERSION@" "woad Library Functions"
.
.
.SH NAME
woad \- minimal ANSI terminal colour codes for C and C++
.
.
.SH LIBRARY
woad (\fB\-lwoad\fR), or none, according to the facilities used; see
\fBNOTES\fR.
.
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <woad/woad.h>
.sp
.B unsigned long woad_version(void);
.B char const *woad_version_string(void);
.sp
.B int woad_colour_policy(void);
.B void woad_refresh(void);
.B int woad_console_supports_ansi(void);
.sp
.BI "int woad_stream_is_tty(FILE *" stm ");"
.BI "int woad_fd_is_tty(int " fd ");"
.BI "int woad_stream_supports_colour(FILE *" stm ");"
.BI "int woad_fd_supports_colour(int " fd ");"
.sp
.BI "char const *woad_seq_for_stream(char const *" seq ", FILE *" stm ");"
.BI "char const *woad_seq_for_fd(char const *" seq ", int " fd ");"
.sp
.B "/* Windows only: */"
.BI "int woad_handle_is_tty(void *" h ");"
.BI "int woad_std_handle_is_tty(unsigned long " id ");"
.BI "int woad_handle_supports_colour(void *" h ");"
.BI "int woad_std_handle_supports_colour(unsigned long " id ");"
.BI "char const *woad_seq_for_handle(char const *" seq ", void *" h ");"
.BI "char const *woad_seq_for_std_handle(char const *" seq ", unsigned long " id ");"
.fi
.
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B woad
provides ANSI SGR (Select Graphic Rendition) colour sequences in two forms:
as fixed string macros, which require no library binding at all, and as
stream-conditional counterparts, which yield a sequence only when colour
should actually be emitted for the stream in question.
.
.SS Fixed sequences
The object-like macros expand to string literals, and so may be concatenated
at compile time and used entirely without linking against the library:
.PP
.RS
.nf
printf(WOAD_FG_RED "error" WOAD_RESET "\en");
.fi
.RE
.PP
The reset sequence is
.BR WOAD_RESET .
The foreground sequences are
.BR WOAD_FG_ \fIC\fR,
and the background sequences
.BR WOAD_BG_ \fIC\fR,
where \fIC\fR is one of
.BR BLACK ,
.BR RED ,
.BR GREEN ,
.BR YELLOW ,
.BR BLUE ,
.BR MAGENTA ,
.BR CYAN ,
or
.BR WHITE .
Each has a bright variant, spelled
.BR WOAD_FG_BRIGHT_ \fIC\fR
and
.BR WOAD_BG_BRIGHT_ \fIC\fR.
There are thus 32 colour macros and one reset macro; for example
.BR WOAD_FG_RED " is " \(dq\e033[31m\(dq ,
and
.BR WOAD_BG_BRIGHT_CYAN " is " \(dq\e033[106m\(dq .
.
.SS Conditional sequences
Each fixed macro has a function-like counterpart, named by suffixing
.BR _FOR ,
which takes the stream for which the sequence is intended and yields either
the sequence or the empty string:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fprintf(stderr, "%serror%s\en", WOAD_FG_RED_FOR(stderr), WOAD_RESET_FOR(stderr));
.fi
.RE
.PP
Since the result is chosen at run time, these are not string literals and
cannot be concatenated; they must be printed, as above, through a conversion
specifier.
.PP
Three further macros take the sequence as a parameter, which is useful when
the colour is itself selected by the caller:
.TP
.BI WOAD_SEQ_FOR( seq ", " stm )
Yields \fIseq\fR if colour should be emitted for \fIstm\fR, else \fB""\fR.
.TP
.BI WOAD_FG_FOR( clr ", " stm ) "\fR, " WOAD_BG_FOR( clr ", " stm )
Spelling variants of
.BR WOAD_SEQ_FOR ,
provided so that the intent of the call site reads correctly; they are
otherwise identical, since a sequence carries its own foreground or
background sense.
.TP
.BI WOAD_RESET_FOR( stm )
Equivalent to
.BI "WOAD_SEQ_FOR(WOAD_RESET, " stm ")\fR."
.
.SS Stream currencies
The
.B _FOR
macros, and the
.B woad_seq_for()
and
.B woad_supports_colour()
wrappers, accept any of the following, dispatching on its type:
.TP
.B FILE *
e.g.
.BR stdout ,
.BR stderr .
.TP
.B int
a file descriptor, e.g. 1 for standard output, 2 for standard error.
.TP
.B void *
[Windows only] a console handle, as from
.BR GetStdHandle() .
.TP
.B unsigned long
[Windows only] a standard-handle identifier, e.g.
.BR STD_ERROR_HANDLE .
.PP
Dispatch is by
.B _Generic
in C11 and later, and by overloading in C++. In earlier C dialects the
wrappers accept
.B FILE *
only, and the other currencies must be reached by naming the specific
function, e.g.
.BR woad_seq_for_fd() .
.
.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B woad_version()
Returns the composite version of the library implementation to which the
caller is bound, with the major version in bits 24\-31, the minor in bits
16\-23, the patch in bits 8\-15, and the pre-release marker in bits 0\-7.
Unlike the
.B WOAD_VER
macro, which records the header the caller compiled against, this is usable
as an ABI probe.
.TP
.B woad_version_string()
Returns the version as a nul-terminated string of the form
.IR M . m . p \fR.
Never NULL; never to be released by the caller.
.TP
.B woad_colour_policy()
Returns the prevailing policy, as determined from the environment:
.BR WOAD_COLOUR_POLICY_AUTO ,
.BR WOAD_COLOUR_POLICY_FORCE ,
or
.BR WOAD_COLOUR_POLICY_INHIBIT .
See
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.TP
.B woad_refresh()
Discards all cached determinations \- colour policy,
.B TERM
inspection, and, on Windows, console capability \- so that the next query
re-evaluates them. Intended for test programs, and for the rare client that
alters its own environment at run time. Not necessary in normal use, and not
safe to call concurrently with other
.B woad
functions.
.TP
.B woad_console_supports_ansi()
Returns non-zero if the attached console is known to interpret ANSI SGR
sequences. On platforms other than Windows this is always non-zero; on
Windows see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.BR woad_stream_is_tty() ", " woad_fd_is_tty()
Return non-zero if the stream or descriptor is attached to a terminal. A
NULL stream yields 0. These exist so that client code need never choose
between
.BR isatty (3),
.BR _isatty() ,
.BR fileno (3),
and the feature-test macros governing which of them are visible.
.TP
.BR woad_stream_supports_colour() ", " woad_fd_supports_colour()
Return non-zero if colour should be emitted, combining the prevailing
policy, the terminal attachment of the stream, and the console's ANSI
capability. A NULL stream yields the result of the policy alone.
.TP
.BR woad_seq_for_stream() ", " woad_seq_for_fd()
Return \fIseq\fR if colour should be emitted for the given stream, else
.BR "" .
A NULL \fIseq\fR is treated as the empty string. The result is never NULL and
never requires release: it is either the caller's own argument or a string
literal, and so remains valid for as long as \fIseq\fR does.
.PP
On Windows,
.BR woad_handle_is_tty() ,
.BR woad_std_handle_is_tty() ,
.BR woad_handle_supports_colour() ,
.BR woad_std_handle_supports_colour() ,
.BR woad_seq_for_handle() ,
and
.BR woad_seq_for_std_handle()
are the handle-based equivalents of the corresponding stream functions. The
handle parameters are declared
.B void *
and
.BR "unsigned long" ,
rather than
.B HANDLE
and
.BR DWORD ,
precisely so that
.I <woad/woad.h>
need not include
.IR <windows.h> .
.
.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The predicates return non-zero for true and 0 for false; they do not fail,
and do not set
.IR errno .
The sequence-returning functions never return NULL. No function in this
library returns storage that the caller must release.
.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The environment is examined once, upon first need, and the result cached for
the lifetime of the process (see
.BR woad_refresh() ).
The following are consulted, in descending order of precedence; the first
that expresses an opinion decides the policy. (One further variable,
.BR WOAD_SET_CONSOLE_MODE ,
governs not the policy but the console, and is described separately below.)
.TP
.BR WOAD_COLOUR ", " WOAD_COLOR
Application-specific override, consulted in that order. A truey value
.RB ( 1 ,
.BR always ,
.BR force ,
.BR on ,
.BR true ,
.BR yes )
forces colour; a falsey value
.RB ( 0 ,
.BR never ,
.BR none ,
.BR off ,
.BR false ,
.BR no )
inhibits it;
.B auto
\- or any unrecognised value \- selects automatic detection. Comparison is
case-insensitive.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set and non-empty, colour is inhibited, as required by the
.UR https://no\-color.org/
NO_COLOR standard
.UE .
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
If set, non-empty, and not
.BR 0 ,
colour is forced.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR
If set to
.BR 0 ,
colour is inhibited.
.TP
.B TERM
If set to
.BR dumb ,
colour is not emitted under automatic detection. This is consulted only
after the above: an explicit force overrides it.
.PP
.B NO_COLOR
is placed above
.BR CLICOLOR_FORCE
because the former standard requires that colour be suppressed when the
variable is present, whereas the application's own variable
.RB ( WOAD_COLOUR )
is placed above both, that standard explicitly reserving to the application
a means of overriding it.
.PP
Separately from the policy:
.TP
.B WOAD_SET_CONSOLE_MODE
[Windows only] Whether
.B woad
may modify the console. A falsey value \- the same set as above \- withholds
permission; absence of the variable grants it, as does any value that is not
falsey.
.IP
Permission is relevant only on those Windows builds for which
.B woad
would otherwise call
.BR SetConsoleMode()
to enable virtual-terminal processing; on Windows 11 (build 22000 and later)
nothing is modified in any case. When permission is withheld, the
determination degenerates to a pure query: a console upon which
virtual-terminal processing is
.I already
enabled is still reported as capable, and one upon which it is not is
reported as incapable rather than being modified.
.IP
This is for the caller who requires that no library alter a console it has
configured itself. To suppress colour, rather than the modification, use
.B NO_COLOR
or
.BR WOAD_COLOUR = never .
.
.
.SH THREAD SAFETY
All functions may be called concurrently, subject to the two qualifications
below.
.PP
.B woad_refresh()
must not be called concurrently with any other
.B woad
function.
.PP
The environment is parsed upon first need. Should several threads arrive at
that point together, each computes the same values and stores them, so the
result is unaffected; strictly, however, those stores are unsynchronised, so
a client that must be provably free of data races \- under
.B ThreadSanitizer
or similar \- should call
.B woad_colour_policy()
once, from a single thread, before becoming multi-threaded.
.PP
On Windows the console determination mutates process-wide state, by way of
.BR SetConsoleMode() ,
and is therefore performed exactly once, with a single thread elected by
compare-and-swap, however many threads call in and however often.
.
.
.SH NOTES
The fixed sequence macros require no binding to the library, so a client
that wants only literal colour codes may copy or include the header alone.
The conditional macros and every function documented here are implemented in
the compiled component, and require
.BR \-lwoad .
.PP
The header includes
.I <stdio.h>
and nothing else \- solely for
.B FILE
\- so that no client translation unit inherits
.IR <windows.h> ,
.IR <io.h> ,
or
.I <unistd.h>
merely by using
.BR woad .
.PP
The once-per-process determinations are once per \fIprocess\fR only when
.B woad
is a shared library; when it is static they are once per binary module, so a
program and a plugin that each link it statically will each perform them.
.PP
On Windows, ANSI capability is settled by inspecting the operating-system
build number and, for builds that require it, attempting to enable
virtual-terminal processing on the standard output and error handles. That
attempt may be withheld; see
.B WOAD_SET_CONSOLE_MODE
under
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Colour that respects the terminal, the policy, and the platform:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include <woad/woad.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    fprintf(stderr, "%swarning%s: %s\en"
    ,   WOAD_FG_YELLOW_FOR(stderr)
    ,   WOAD_RESET_FOR(stderr)
    ,   "the roof is on fire");

    return 0;
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compile and link, and then observe the policy at work:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cc \-o warn warn.c \-lwoad
$ ./warn                          # coloured, if stderr is a terminal
$ ./warn 2>&1 | cat               # not coloured: stderr is a pipe
$ WOAD_COLOUR=always ./warn 2>&1 | cat  # coloured regardless
$ NO_COLOR=1 ./warn               # not coloured, terminal or not
.fi
.RE
.
.
.SH CONFORMING TO
The sequences are those of ECMA\-48 (ISO/IEC 6429) SGR, as universally
implemented by ANSI/VT-compatible terminals.
.B NO_COLOR
is handled as required by the informal standard published at
.UR https://no\-color.org/
no\-color.org
.UE .
.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <woad/woad.h>
The sole header.
.TP
.I libwoad.a\fR, \fIlibwoad.so
The compiled component.
.TP
.I woad.pc
.BR pkg\-config (1)
metadata; see
.BR "pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs woad" .
.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR isatty (3),
.BR fileno (3),
.BR printf (3),
.BR pkg\-config (1),
.BR terminfo (5)
.PP
Full documentation, including the rationale for the compiled component, is at
.UR https://github.com/synesissoftware/woad/
the woad repository
.UE .
.
.
